From: dresden196 <50474030+dresden196@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] NickelOS host apps are their own kind of app, not Crostini's

NickelOS's Linux apps run on the device itself. nickel-appd registered
them in the GuestOS registry as Crostini apps (VM type TERMINA, VM
"nickel"), so everywhere outside the paths 0002 bends they were treated as
apps in a Crostini VM. Now they have their own types (docs/host-apps.md in
the NickelOS overlay):

- system_api (Chrome's copy; platform2's has the same patch):
  vm_tools.apps.VmType NICKEL_HOST = 100, high so that a type ChromiumOS
  adds later does not take it.
- App Service: AppType::kNickelHost (also in the app_types proto, value
  100, and the App Management mojom), with its own publisher,
  NickelHostApps, a GuestOSApps on the pattern of the Crostini one:
  allowed whenever host apps are (crostini::IsNickelNativeAppsAllowed),
  launching through crostini::LaunchCrostiniApp, which hands VM "nickel"
  to nickel-appd (0002), not shown in App Management yet.
- GuestOS registry: NICKEL_HOST apps are enabled with host apps and
  handle links through the same launch path; guest_os::ToAppType maps
  the VM type to the app type.
- Every switch over app types and the comparisons that set a window's
  type and the launcher's behavior treat kNickelHost like kCrostini, so
  host apps behave as before. Two differences: they get no "Linux apps"
  launcher folder, and Files offers them for "Open with" without asking
  whether the files can be shared with a VM.

App ids are unchanged: nickel-appd keeps VM name "nickel" and container
"host", from which the ids are derived.

